{"authored" [{"question" "q-37083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y).$ What is the magnitude of the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-37083/review.json" "file_sha256" "c72c3317265c24fc55f0c835febaa187f5c14f3b9c0ad1bdfa821351378b3f6e" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-37083/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "98c06a5125a042ec653fd6cd535ff07d3c6efe3f26f550885bdfdcc8e87d47e7" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-37083/verification.json" "basis" 2395 "rationale" "For f=(x+y)ln(x+y), grad=(ln(x+y)+1,ln(x+y)+1); at(2,−1) grad=(1,1). Negative unit gradient gives signedrate−sqrt2, magnitude sqrt2. Source accepted positive sqrt2, so minimally clarify requested magnitude, prefix the retained original source calculation, and append the signed-rate note. All five original choices/answer/field IDs and source key remain unchanged; source artifacts, full original solution and historical version remain retained. Correct only the two missing minuses in the negative-gradient coefficient equality, and label its final norm calculation as magnitude."} {"question" "q-37083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Interpret the positive quantity calculated below as the magnitude of the rate of change in the direction of fastest decrease.\n\nAt a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(2,-1) & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction of $-∇f(2,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(2,-1)‖ & =‖-i-j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSigned-rate note: $\\nabla f(2,-1)=(1,1)$ and the unit direction of fastest decrease is $\\mathbf u=(-1,-1)/\\sqrt2$. Therefore $D_{\\mathbf u}f(2,-1)=\\nabla f(2,-1)\\cdot\\mathbf u=-\\sqrt2$. Its magnitude is $\\sqrt2$, which is the positive quantity requested in the clarified study question." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-37083/review.json" "file_sha256" "c72c3317265c24fc55f0c835febaa187f5c14f3b9c0ad1bdfa821351378b3f6e" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-37083/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "98c06a5125a042ec653fd6cd535ff07d3c6efe3f26f550885bdfdcc8e87d47e7" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-37083/verification.json" "basis" 2395 "rationale" "For f=(x+y)ln(x+y), grad=(ln(x+y)+1,ln(x+y)+1); at(2,−1) grad=(1,1). Negative unit gradient gives signedrate−sqrt2, magnitude sqrt2. Source accepted positive sqrt2, so minimally clarify requested magnitude, prefix the retained original source calculation, and append the signed-rate note. All five original choices/answer/field IDs and source key remain unchanged; source artifacts, full original solution and historical version remain retained. Correct only the two missing minuses in the negative-gradient coefficient equality, and label its final norm calculation as magnitude."} {"question" "q-37083" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-37083/review.json" "file_sha256" "c72c3317265c24fc55f0c835febaa187f5c14f3b9c0ad1bdfa821351378b3f6e" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-37083/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "98c06a5125a042ec653fd6cd535ff07d3c6efe3f26f550885bdfdcc8e87d47e7" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-37083/verification.json" "basis" 2395 "rationale" "For f=(x+y)ln(x+y), grad=(ln(x+y)+1,ln(x+y)+1); at(2,−1) grad=(1,1). Negative unit gradient gives signedrate−sqrt2, magnitude sqrt2. Source accepted positive sqrt2, so minimally clarify requested magnitude, prefix the retained original source calculation, and append the signed-rate note. All five original choices/answer/field IDs and source key remain unchanged; source artifacts, full original solution and historical version remain retained. Correct only the two missing minuses in the negative-gradient coefficient equality, and label its final norm calculation as magnitude."} {"question" "q-86429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=6{x}^{2}+2y$ at the point $(0,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=3i+5j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}}=\\sqrt{34} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}(3i+5j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+2y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+2y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =12x\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(0,1) & =(12\\cdot 0)\\,i+2\\,j \\\\  & =2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇\\,f(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =2j\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}i+\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{34}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 5}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{10\\sqrt{43}}{34}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11\\sqrt{34}}{34}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11\\sqrt{43}}{43}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{34}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y$ at the point $A(1,0)$ in the direction towards the point $B(0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨0,1⟩-⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{y}+2xy,\\,x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,0) & =⟨{e}^{0}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 0,\\,1\\cdot {e}^{0}+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,0) & =∇f(1,0)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ at the point $(0,-1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=3\\,i+4\\,j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}j \\\\ ∇\\,f(0,-1) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2(-1)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(0,-1) & =∇\\,f(0,-1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =-2\\,j\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(4)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c362cd907fb85830ab3320317ae756b56808309a5fb6a6d98274061b8fc74a3f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type