se_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-83554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy$ increase most rapidly at the point $(-1,1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-83554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-y)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{x}{y}+{e}^{x}-x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1\\cdot {e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{-1}{1}+{e}^{-1}-(-1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/state.json" "file_sha256" "abc84f7aef8805102238c63265050a108257d205b44bbf08f3fcd9e3a1c4529f" "basis" 2394 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Aca